## Questions
1. Why did the group stop at 2 instances?
   The group stopped at 2 instances because the maximum capacity was set to 2. This prevents Auto Scaling from launching more than the allowed number of instances.
2. Why did terminating an instance by hand not remove the cost?
   Terminating an instance manually did not remove the cost because the Auto Scaling Group automatically launched a replacement to maintain its desired capacity of 2 instances.
3. Why is the target value set to your assigned value (e.g., 30-85 percent) instead of 99 percent?
   The target value is assigned to each group to provide different CPU utilization thresholds. Setting it to 99% would allow CPU utilization to become too high before triggering scaling, which could affect application performance.
4. What did the automatic cutoff protect us from?
   The automatic cutoff protected us from leaving AWS resources running longer than necessary, which could result in additional charges.
5. What changes when a load balancer sits in front of the group?
   A load balancer distributes incoming traffic across available instances instead of requiring users to access each instance through its individual public IP address. It also helps direct traffic to healthy instances when the group scales up or replaces an instance.
